            <description><![CDATA[<!-- Snappy 1.2.1 release notes. -->
<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>Snappy 1.2.1</title>
<style>
  :root {
    color-scheme: light dark;
    --ink: #16181b;
    --mid: #63696f;
    --surface: #ffffff;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root {
      --ink: #e8e9eb;
      --mid: #9aa0a6;
      --surface: #191b1f;
    }
  }
  body {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 16px 18px 22px;
    font: 13px/1.55 -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "SF Pro Text", system-ui, sans-serif;
    color: var(--ink);
    background: var(--surface);
  }
  h1 { font-size: 17px; margin: 0 0 2px; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  h2 { font-size: 13px; margin: 20px 0 6px; letter-spacing: 0.02em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--mid); }
  p { margin: 0 0 9px; }
  ul { margin: 0 0 9px; padding-left: 19px; }
  li { margin-bottom: 5px; }
  .version-date { color: var(--mid); margin: 0 0 4px; }
</style>

<h1>Snappy 1.2.2</h1>
<p class="version-date">Window placement for macOS</p>

<h2>New</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Drag to place on any display.</strong> The placement grid now opens
    on every connected display at once. Draw a region on whichever screen you
    want the window on and it moves there — across displays as well as within
    one. Previously the grid appeared only on the display holding the window, so
    a drag could never move a window to another screen. Keyboard placements are
    unchanged: they still act on the display the window is already on.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>Fixed</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Memory that was never released.</strong> Each time the placement
    grid had to be built afresh, the previous one was kept in memory rather than
    freed, so Snappy’s footprint grew slowly for as long as it stayed running. It
    is now released as intended. If Snappy has been running for a long time, this
    update restarts it and reclaims that memory.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>Notes</h2>
<ul>
  <li>No change to permissions or your settings.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
